00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:25.480
Six months to go.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.120
Sometimes you can almost feel the clock ticking

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.200
and that's not a good thing.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.840
Feeling the clock meant waking up with short-timer syndrome

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.160
tugging at me.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.040
Thanks, but no thanks.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.160
In response, I just doubled down on my routine.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.560
I tried to keep in my lane

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.800
and let as much of the prison bullshit as possible

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.480
roll off my back.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.320
What's that joke?

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:50.880
How do you make God laugh?

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.920
You tell him your plans?

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.480
Well, it's the same thing in prison.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.360
One of the first lessons I learned

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.360
on my first day at Loretto

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.800
was that the officials running the prison could

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.880
and can do virtually anything they want

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.560
to the prisoners under their charge.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.000
The prisoners have zero recourse

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.080
to do anything whatsoever about it.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:15.160
Now, officially, there's an administrative remedy process.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.360
That's supposed to allow prisoners the opportunity

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.200
to challenge wrongs against them

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.680
and to have them corrected,

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.560
like when their constitutional rights are being trampled.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.640
But the remedy process is as corrupt

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.200
as the Bureau of Prisons is

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.960
and that's saying something.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.240
For starters, the BOP regularly ignores its own rules

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.520
and they definitely ignore their own rules

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.280
as they pertain to the administrative remedy process.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.920
For instance, the BOP refuses to abide

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.240
by the time restrictions placed on it for responses.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:45.640
And I've never heard of anybody

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.800
ever, ever winning an appeal.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.520
In fact, the whole purpose

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.680
is to give the BOP a fig leaf

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.760
so they can throw out every single prisoner complaint

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.320
based on technicalities,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.680
usually that the prisoner didn't file in time.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.320
It's always nonsense.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.720
For the BOP, the whole cynical point of the exercise

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.880
is the hope that the prisoners lose interest

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.960
in pursuing justice for themselves

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.680
and just simply give up.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.480
Or the remedy process takes so long

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.640
and demands so much hoop jumping

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.080
that the prisoner gets released

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:18.680
having served their sentence

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:22.320
and their appeal dies having never even made it to court.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.360
Even more cynically,

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.720
it's all just a paperwork exercise

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.560
to pretend to show the courts

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:28.960
that the BOP is doing something

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.000
to address prisoner grievances.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.760
For prisoners, it's just another sick joke.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.440
As I told you at the very beginning

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:36.680
of this part of the story,

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:38.600
when I arrived at Loretto,

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.560
I expected to be placed in the minimum security camp,

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:44.080
literally across the street from the prison.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.880
But it turned out that the Bureau of Prisons

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:50.520
gave me a PSF, a public safety factor designation.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.360
That meant that I was considered

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.880
to be so dangerous by the BOP

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.520
and such a threat to the public safety

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.280
that I would never be allowed to serve

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.560
any of my sentence in a minimum security camp,

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:04.680
not a single day.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.320
That was despite the fact that my sentencing judge,

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.520
a Clinton appointee with 20 years experience on the bench,

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.040
had recommended that I serve my time

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:14.640
in a minimum security camp.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.480
And it was despite the fact

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:19.120
that the US attorney who represented the government

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.840
in its case against me also recommended camp,

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.320
not prison.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.720
Neither of the people who put me in prison

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.720
believed I was a threat to the public.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:31.200
In fact, nobody in any position of authority

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.240
believed I was a threat to anyone.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:34.640
And yet, apparently,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.520
some nameless, faceless, bureaucratic half-wit

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.240
in the BOP's Grand Prairie Texas Designation

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.140
and Sentence Computation Center,

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:47.040
the DSCC decided, regardless that I was a threat,

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.200
discerning the same public safety designation

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.280
as murderers, members of organized crime,

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:54.000
and child molesters.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.000
I appealed this stupid decision,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.960
but I didn't knowing that it would do nothing for me.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.600
The Bureau of Prisons had deemed me dangerous,

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.520
simply because they could.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:04.840
And there was little to nothing

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.760
that I could do to counter it.

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:10.720
How a country or a culture punishes people

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.960
speaks volumes about the country or the culture.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:17.160
Ironically, I wasn't anywhere near as dangerous

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.440
as the Bureau of Prisons is to prisoners

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.400
and to public safety itself.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.560
But then, if I'm 100% honest,

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:25.440
the BOP was not wrong about me.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.240
They weren't wrong to suspect that I was dangerous.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.760
It's just that I was dangerous to them.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:35.000
I'm John Kiriakou.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.240
Welcome to Dead Drop.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.010
What makes a spy tick?

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:42.930
This is episode 12 in our series,

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:44.550
Doing Time Like a Spy.

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:47.050
Before we get back to Loretto,

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:48.450
I wanna thank you, as always,

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:50.010
for listening to the podcast

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.290
and for all of your wonderful comments.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:54.370
Thank you as well for liking, rating,

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.910
and reviewing us wherever you listen.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.290
In the world of podcasting,

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.250
you, the listener, are incredibly powerful

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:02.810
and important to us.

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:04.250
So thank you for being there.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:06.490
What the DSCC,

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:10.010
the Designation and Sentence Computation Center did,

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:12.690
placing a public safety factor designation on me,

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.010
was just simply bullshit.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:18.170
Their excuse was that I had committed espionage

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:21.090
and espionage along with treason, sabotage,

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:22.450
and other related offenses fall

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:25.770
within the so-called greatest security scale,

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.130
therefore warranting such a design.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:29.850
But as my attorneys pointed out,

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:31.370
I had been convicted of violating

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:33.250
the Intelligence Identities Protection Act,

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:36.750
which has nothing whatsoever to do with espionage.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.770
It's not even in the same part of the US code

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:41.810
as the US Espionage Act.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:43.650
That was one of the reasons that the judge

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.970
and the prosecutors had recommended camp

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:47.450
instead of prison.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:50.250
The DSCC ignored my attorney's letter

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:51.690
and instead sent it to the warden

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:53.690
at Loretto for a response.

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:56.930
This was cynical and disingenuous.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.890
Now, I knew the warden at Loretto.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.050
In fact, he was one of the few people there

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:04.930
whom I at least semi-respected.

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:07.770
He was a man of his word, usually,

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:09.970
and this designation was not his word.

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:12.090
I should also mention that crimes like treason,

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:16.570
espionage, and sabotage often carry the death penalty.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:20.390
I was sentenced to 30 months, reducible to 23 months.

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:22.790
How dangerous could I really have been

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:25.570
in their own honest estimations?

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.470
The warden, man of his word, though he was,

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.790
insisted that he could not reverse the decision.

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:33.550
In fact, he parroted my lawyer's words

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:35.710
right back to us while ignoring the facts.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:38.510
My attorneys had written that the PSF,

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.230
the Public Safety Factor, applies to, quote,

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:43.310
espionage, treason, sabotage,

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:45.730
and other related offenses, unquote.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:47.510
The warden had claimed in his response

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:50.390
that my crime was an other related offense.

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:51.390
I appealed that.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:55.070
But then the BOP's regional director waited in.

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:58.150
He wrote, quote, your total offensive behavior

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:01.150
is to be considered utilizing the most serious offense

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:04.950
or act committed when determining offense security level.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:07.030
Boom, the cat was out of the bag.

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:11.510
The security designation has nothing to do with your crime.

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:13.990
It has to do with whatever the government

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:17.350
originally accused you of doing,

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:21.310
whether the charges were dropped or thrown out or not.

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:26.390
The government at then CIA director David Petraeus' insistence

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:28.870
had piled all kinds of bullshit charges on me,

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:30.490
including espionage.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:33.710
So holy shit, what happened to due process here?

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:36.090
If I had actually committed the crimes I was charged with,

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.750
why didn't the government then insist on going to trial?

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:41.510
Why did they make five different plea offers

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:43.670
because they knew it was all bullshit?

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:47.390
Why did they drop four of the five charges?

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:49.350
Obviously, because they were bullshit.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:51.230
And if I was so dangerous,

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:53.710
then why didn't the BOP put me

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:55.790
in a maximum security penitentiary?

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:57.910
Why in a low security prison?

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:00.950
Until I received that letter from the BOP,

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:03.550
I had believed that many of the BOP's officials

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:05.830
were either lazy or stupid.

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:07.890
How wrong I was.

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:10.190
What they are is malicious

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:12.490
in addition to being lazy and stupid,

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:14.210
but malicious above all.

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:16.890
Now, part of the problem here was that so many people

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:20.410
in powerful positions had no idea what espionage is.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:22.010
Certainly not by definition.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:24.850
So when they talk about it and throw the word around,

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:26.930
the word is utterly meaningless.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:28.010
And it gets worse.

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.370
If they had done an ounce of due diligence

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:33.730
and say checked the government's own records,

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:36.930
they would have seen that the only other person

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:40.930
who had ever been convicted of violating the IIPA,

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:42.890
the law that they say I broke,

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:45.050
and five counts of it, by the way,

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:48.690
served nine months of a five-year sentence

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:49.890
at Camp Cupcake.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:52.170
So even if I had committed espionage,

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:53.770
precedent was on my side.

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:56.490
But alas, I'm gonna use that word again, bullshit,

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:59.810
great steaming piles of it was on their side.

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.490
It turned out the BOP was punishing me all along

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:05.490
for exercising my right to freedom of speech.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:08.810
I guess that's what comes from being dangerous,

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:12.170
except it's the BOP that's being dangerous.

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:13.450
In the episodes ahead,

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:16.290
you're gonna meet a terrific man named Paul Wright,

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:17.570
a former prisoner himself.

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:19.570
Paul co-created something called

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:21.570
Prison Legal News Magazine.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:24.290
It's the longest running newspaper or magazine

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:27.690
produced by and for current and former prisoners

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:29.130
in American history.

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:33.210
In a July 2014 article published in Prison Legal News

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:36.530
entitled BOP Grievance System Contributes

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:39.170
to Defiance of Compliance by Prisoners

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:42.530
about a study conducted by the US Marshall Service

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:45.450
and the Department of Criminology and Criminal Justice

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.050
at the University of Maryland,

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:50.690
found that, quote, the failure of BOP officials

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:52.690
to adequately respond to grievances

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:56.210
contributes to higher levels of violence

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:57.930
in federal prisons.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:11:59.810
The research study determined

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:02.370
that a benefit of the BOP's grievance system

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:05.930
is deflecting or reducing potential litigation.

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:08.010
Indeed, many federal court decisions

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:10.290
have been decided in the BOP's favor

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.090
based upon prisoners' failures

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:15.410
to exhaust administrative remedies, unquote.

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:18.290
The article went on to say that, quote,

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:20.490
the BOP's grievance system is perceived

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:23.210
by some prisoners as overly formal

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:26.570
and more concerned with procedural practices

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:29.610
and deadlines than the substance of a complaint.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.250
Accordingly, data suggests that a higher volume

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:35.010
of late or rejected grievance responses

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:36.450
will increase violence.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.570
Two features of the grievance process

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:40.570
consistently predicted violence,

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:43.090
the proportion of responses which relate

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:44.970
and the proportion of responses

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:47.330
which were substantively rejected.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:50.050
So is the BOP really worried about violence here

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:52.210
or are they worried about maintaining a regimen

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:54.810
of bullshit and corruption?

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:56.850
This behavior by the BOP,

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:59.050
saying they do things to reduce violence

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:02.090
while in fact doing things that increase violence

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:05.490
is pervasive, systemic, and far-reaching.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:06.610
As I've said here,

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:07.930
though one loses their freedom

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:09.290
when sentenced to prison or jail,

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:12.970
one does not lose their constitutional rights.

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:15.570
Federal prisoners have a constitutional right

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:17.370
to adequate medical care.

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:18.690
That right is guaranteed

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:20.490
under the Eighth Amendment's prohibition

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.970
against cruel and unusual punishment.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:26.050
That means that the BOP is legally obligated

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:27.970
to provide essential physical,

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:30.290
dental, and mental health services.

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:32.010
The US Supreme Court established

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:34.210
in the case of Estelle versus Gamble

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:38.730
that ignoring serious medical needs is unconstitutional.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:41.050
There's a lot of gray here to be sure.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:42.890
What is essential care?

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:46.170
When does the obligation to provide it kick in?

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:48.250
The default response from prison authorities

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:50.650
to any prisoner expressing physical illness

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:54.050
or disability of any kind is cynicism.

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:56.090
That's the starting point for prisoner healthcare.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:13:57.370
You're lying.

00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:00.530
The Second Circuit has held that the denial of care

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:02.490
has to be objectively serious enough

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:05.370
to create a condition of urgency.

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:07.450
That is, a situation where death,

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:09.970
permanent injury, or extreme pain

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:12.930
appears likely to occur or has occurred.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:15.570
On the plus side, recent court decisions

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:18.050
have emphasized pain and disability

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:20.570
when evaluating prisoners' medical needs.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:21.850
With all of that in mind,

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:23.650
in February 2015,

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:26.610
as many as 2,800 prisoners

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:29.690
at the Willisey County Correctional Center in Texas

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:32.050
seized control of the prison

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:35.650
to protest the low quality of medical care there.

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:37.290
According to the Associated Press,

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:40.050
quote, inmates reported that their medical concerns

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:41.930
were often ignored by staff

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:43.730
and that corners were often cut

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:45.930
when it came to healthcare, unquote.

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:48.930
An attorney with the Texas Civil Rights Project

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.930
said that he, quote, was not surprised

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:54.890
inadequate medical care could ignite a riot.

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:57.770
It's pretty abysmal with regard to modern standards

00:14:57.770 --> 00:15:00.250
of how people should be treated, unquote.

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:02.130
What any normal person would consider

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:04.610
medical malpractice outside prison?

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:07.970
Well, it's a common occurrence in prison.

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:11.010
My bunkmate, Frank, was 65 years old.

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:14.130
He had diabetes and a history of heart trouble.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:15.530
He was insulin dependent

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:19.610
and took enough medications to stock a mid-sized pharmacy.

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:21.170
We were sitting in our room one evening

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:23.770
when he suddenly felt a tightness in his chest.

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:26.250
When a 65-year-old guy with health issues says

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.010
his chest feels tight, you run and get help.

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:31.810
Together we went to see the CO on duty in our unit.

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:34.970
Frank told the CO about the sudden tightness in his chest

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:36.370
and that it was coming on top of the day

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:38.410
spent being short of breath.

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:40.610
One plus one equals two, right?

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:42.690
Nothing I can do, said the CO.

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:44.650
That's what he said, nothing I can do.

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:49.290
Sick call was from six to 615 a.m. Monday through Friday.

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:50.130
You're gonna have to wait

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:52.410
and go to medical services the next morning.

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:55.010
So one plus one equals nothing.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:56.450
That's what Frank did.

00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:58.610
He stood in line for medical services,

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:02.050
finally got seen and was given Tylenol.

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:04.930
One plus one equals less than nothing.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:08.210
But the physician's assistant on duty did tell Frank

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:10.530
that he, the physician's assistant,

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:13.170
would do something proactive on Frank's behalf.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:16.570
He would apply to the regional medical officer in Pittsburgh

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:19.610
to approve a request to do an EKG.

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:21.290
Okay, thought Frank.

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:26.290
One plus one equals, well, still nothing it turned out.

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:27.890
Months passed.

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:31.810
The tightening in Frank's chest did not pass.

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:33.570
The tightening got worse.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:36.130
Frank would tell me sometimes out of breath

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:37.690
and worried that it felt like

00:16:37.690 --> 00:16:39.730
somebody was sitting on his chest.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:40.930
Or maybe that's what it feels like

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:43.130
when the BOP is sitting on your chest.

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:46.810
Am I urging Frank returned to medical services?

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:49.450
The physician's assistant insisted still

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:51.330
that there was nothing he could do.

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:53.170
It was all up to the regional office

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:55.490
and the heartless bureaucrats there.

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:58.530
Another two months went by.

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:01.530
One morning, Frank got up to go early

00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:02.610
to the insulin line.

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:04.090
I slept in that morning.

00:17:04.090 --> 00:17:06.450
I got up at eight and I headed to the shower.

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:07.650
On my way over to the shower,

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:09.450
a prisoner who lived near the front of the unit

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:10.410
called out to me,

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:12.130
yo, John, he says,

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:14.010
Frank had a heart attack and insulin line.

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:16.770
He just dropped to the ground, unconscious.

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:18.890
I was like, what the fuck?

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:20.890
I felt dizzy with anger.

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:23.050
Frank had been telling the medical unit

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:25.330
literally for months

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:28.210
that he was having a serious cardiac health issue.

00:17:28.210 --> 00:17:30.170
The classic signs of a man

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:34.330
either having a heart attack or speeding toward one.

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:37.730
The COs, as I've said, are mostly idiots.

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:39.330
Of course they would ignore it.

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:41.730
But a health official?

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:44.210
Even a quasi-health official?

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:46.690
Physicians assistants like those at Loretto

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:49.050
do not take the Hippocratic Oath.

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:52.690
But they do take a distinct profession-specific pledge

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:56.250
known as the Physician Assistant Professional Oath.

00:17:56.250 --> 00:17:57.970
No matter how you cut it,

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:00.090
no matter what metric you use,

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:01.770
everyone at Loretto failed to meet

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:08.780
their most basic professional obligations here.

