Now back to Dead Drop.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:00.160
Frank survived the heart attack.

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:03.200
He spent 11 days in a local hospital,

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:05.120
chained to a hospital bed,

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:08.280
then underwent surgery for two angioplasties

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:10.480
and to install two stents.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:12.560
Why not bypass surgery?

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:15.600
A better, more holistic solution to Frank's medical issue.

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:17.960
Too expensive, said the BOP,

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:20.640
which ended up transferring Frank to a prison hospital

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:22.000
in Massachusetts.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:27.000
A 15 hour drive away from his family and his support.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:28.480
Then there was Larry.

00:21:28.480 --> 00:21:30.080
I didn't know Larry very well.

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:32.360
He lived near the chapel where I worked as an orderly

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:33.800
so ours was a,

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:36.240
hey Larry, how you doing kind of relationship?

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:38.600
One day I passed Larry on my way to work

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:40.440
and he's visibly hunched over.

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:41.760
I said, you okay?

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:44.920
Like one normal human being to another.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:47.300
He told me that his back was killing him.

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:48.720
It had been for over a year.

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.380
In fact, he had gone to sit call that morning

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:52.920
where they just shrugged it off as usual

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:55.680
and they gave him the usual Tylenol.

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:59.240
Seriously, the man with visible physical impact

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:01.880
on his frame complained about the same health issue

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:04.000
for over a year.

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:07.240
The effect on Larry was profound and it was getting worse.

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:09.800
Next time I bumped into him a few weeks later

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:11.680
he was walking with a cane.

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:13.160
His pain was that bad.

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:16.200
It was that chronic, that evident.

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:18.840
By now the Tylenol was utterly useless.

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:21.000
It wasn't putting a dent in his pain.

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:22.240
I told him, buddy,

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:24.600
you need to have your family call your congressman.

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:26.520
He needed heavyweight help.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:28.360
That's how far this had escalated.

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:31.080
But actually finding and getting heavyweight help

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:32.840
is beyond most people.

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:34.400
It was beyond Larry.

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:35.720
A little later I was attending

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:38.160
the chapel weekly staff meeting.

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:39.920
The chaplain mentioned that he was going to stop

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:41.960
by Larry's cell to see how he was doing.

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:45.200
It turned out that Larry had gone from needing a cane

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:46.800
to needing a walker,

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:49.940
to needing a wheelchair to get around.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:53.000
I told the chaplain about his persistent back pain,

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:54.760
about his years long attempts

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:57.800
to get any sort of actual medical intervention.

00:22:57.800 --> 00:22:59.360
I asked the chaplain to intervene

00:22:59.360 --> 00:23:01.400
if he could on Larry's behalf.

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:05.280
And the chaplain did intervene to his great credit.

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:08.180
He went to medical and he demanded

00:23:08.180 --> 00:23:11.080
that Larry be taken to an outside hospital for testing.

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:13.320
Another two weeks went by

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:14.680
before the regional medical office

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:17.080
finally agreed to pay for the test.

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:19.520
They took Larry to an actual hospital

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:21.360
where he underwent an MRI.

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:23.960
And they diagnosed him with stage four

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:26.080
cancer of the spine.

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:27.520
They sent him back to Loretto

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:30.060
where he died in his bunk two weeks later,

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:34.520
having never undergone any treatment of any kind

00:23:34.520 --> 00:23:36.320
except for Tylenol.

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:40.160
He was never considered for any sort of compassionate release.

00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:43.520
In a lot of ways Larry was ignored to death.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:47.360
Look, there are a lot of genuinely bad people at Loretto

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:50.240
but none of them had been sentenced to death.

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:51.720
Not literally anyway.

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:54.160
They deserve and continue to deserve

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:56.800
at least basic medical care.

00:23:56.800 --> 00:24:00.380
A reminder, the BOP is legally obligated

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:03.200
to provide essential health services.

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:06.840
Ignoring serious medical needs is unconstitutional.

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:09.640
And if minding the constitution is too big a lift

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:12.080
and too hard, how about even just showing

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:15.320
a modicum of human compassion?

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:17.720
I described my own continuing problems

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:20.160
with the medical unit in a letter from Loretto

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:22.840
dated September 12th, 2014.

00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:24.280
This is what I said.

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:26.760
Hello again from the Federal Correctional Institution

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:28.560
at Loretto, Pennsylvania.

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:30.240
I had a scare a few weeks ago

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:31.320
that I wanted to tell you about

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:34.920
because it's so typical of the BOP experience.

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:37.800
By way of background, I have type two diabetes

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:40.440
although I am not insulin dependent.

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:44.800
A month ago, I had a routine quarterly blood test.

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:46.920
I have no idea what, if anything,

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:49.560
that blood test revealed as my physician's assistant

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:52.720
never put me on a call out to inform me.

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:54.720
A call out is a scheduled appointment.

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:57.600
A few days later I was put on a call out

00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:59.260
but it was to the pharmacy,

00:24:59.260 --> 00:25:01.180
not to the physician's assistant.

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:03.060
I went to the appointment and the pharmacist

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:04.620
handed me a bottle of pills.

00:25:04.620 --> 00:25:06.300
What is this? I asked.

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:07.940
Gliburide, he said.

00:25:07.940 --> 00:25:09.980
Like the name should mean something to me.

00:25:09.980 --> 00:25:11.220
I said, what's it for?

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:12.300
Diabetes.

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:14.020
Only insulin dependent diabetics

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:15.860
are allowed to have testing meters.

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:20.460
So I had no idea what my blood sugar numbers were.

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:21.980
So now I'm getting worried.

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:24.020
Do I have a problem? I asked.

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:25.480
I don't know, he said.

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:26.780
Your PA called it in.

00:25:26.780 --> 00:25:28.500
He'll put you on call out.

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:30.060
I took the pills back to my room.

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:31.780
The instructions on the bottle directed me

00:25:31.780 --> 00:25:33.820
to take a pill a day with food.

00:25:33.820 --> 00:25:35.340
So that's what I did.

00:25:35.340 --> 00:25:37.900
At 2 a.m. on the second day,

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:41.060
I woke up in my bunk drenched in sweat.

00:25:41.060 --> 00:25:44.820
I got down off my bunk, my head just swimming.

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:47.700
I got my bearings enough to stumble to my locker

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:49.020
to eat a cookie.

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:52.540
When your heads like that, even thinking can be hard

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:54.820
but I had reasoned that my blood sugar

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:56.940
had dropped dangerously low.

00:25:56.940 --> 00:25:59.740
The cookie made me feel better almost immediately.

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:02.300
I drank some water and then I went back to bed.

00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:03.940
The next morning I felt fine.

00:26:03.940 --> 00:26:05.700
That evening with dinner,

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:07.940
I took the next dose of gliburri.

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:10.620
I went to bed at 11 and I slept through the night.

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:14.860
I woke up at seven, again soaked in sweat.

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:17.340
I lowered myself onto a chair,

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:19.620
overwhelmed by the urge to vomit,

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:20.800
but then it passed.

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:23.100
I went to the bathroom and washed my face.

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:26.260
The bathroom is about 100 feet from my cubicle

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:28.480
and I started to make my way up there.

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:30.640
After a few steps, I got dizzy.

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:32.960
I heard someone say, John, are you okay?

00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:35.960
I mumbled, I'm not sure what's happening to me.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:38.480
A friend and fellow prisoner named Waheed,

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:40.080
who was a pharmacist on the street,

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:43.680
said, your blood sugar's crashing, sit down.

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:45.440
I sat down on the concrete floor

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:47.560
while Waheed ran and got a coke.

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.420
I took two sips and then just everything went gray

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:52.320
and I passed out on the floor.

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:54.760
I woke up 15 minutes later in a wheelchair

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:56.000
in the medical unit.

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.600
A nurse helped me onto an examination table

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:00.200
and took my blood pressure.

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:02.520
It was 88 over 60.

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:04.240
He took it a second time.

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:06.780
Again, it was 88 over 60.

00:27:06.780 --> 00:27:09.220
He asked me what medications I was taking.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:11.080
I told him about the gliburide

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:13.720
and he suggested rather directly

00:27:13.720 --> 00:27:16.300
that, quote, perhaps you're not taking it

00:27:16.300 --> 00:27:19.260
the way you're supposed to, unquote.

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:21.920
I assured him that I was

00:27:21.920 --> 00:27:25.580
and that I had only taken three pills in three days.

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:27.300
The nurse then suggested that I passed out

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:29.500
because I was dehydrated.

00:27:29.500 --> 00:27:32.620
Okay, I said, are you gonna give me an IV?

00:27:32.620 --> 00:27:35.700
No, he said, we don't do that.

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:38.460
Let's do an EKG to make sure you didn't have a heart attack.

00:27:38.460 --> 00:27:40.100
The EKG was normal.

00:27:40.100 --> 00:27:41.980
I told the nurse that I was certain

00:27:41.980 --> 00:27:43.860
that this was a case of low blood sugar

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:45.340
because of, let me do that again.

00:27:45.340 --> 00:27:46.860
I told the nurse that I was certain

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:48.460
this was a case of low blood sugar

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:50.260
because of the gliburide.

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:53.020
He tested my blood, which was 138.

00:27:53.060 --> 00:27:56.780
Normal fasting levels should be between 70 and 100.

00:27:56.780 --> 00:27:59.660
I told him about the two sips of coke I had had,

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:01.860
which would then account for the higher level.

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:04.140
He went back to the dehydration diagnosis

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:06.180
and took my blood pressure twice more,

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:09.140
90 over 60, 90 over 60.

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:11.300
The nurse told me to sit in a chair in the hall

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:13.720
and when the regularly scheduled move was called

00:28:13.720 --> 00:28:16.180
at 825 a.m., I should go back to my unit

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:19.460
and, quote, take it easy for the day, unquote.

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:20.980
I never saw a doctor.

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:24.100
I never even saw a physician's assistant.

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:25.220
That was three weeks ago

00:28:25.220 --> 00:28:28.300
and I still have not seen a medical professional.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:30.580
I've mentioned before that my father-in-law

00:28:30.580 --> 00:28:32.220
is a highly respected physician

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:34.820
and former pharmacist in Cincinnati.

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:36.780
I called him immediately.

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:39.140
Stop the gliburide, he said.

00:28:39.140 --> 00:28:42.320
Gliburide is a medication meant as a last resort

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:44.420
before diabetics go in insulin

00:28:44.420 --> 00:28:46.100
and I'm not at that stage.

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:49.060
Besides, gliburide can ruin the pancreas.

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:52.560
That's why so many physicians refuse to prescribe it.

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:55.120
He added that if I continued the gliburide,

00:28:55.120 --> 00:28:57.940
I could expect to eventually go into heart failure.

00:28:57.940 --> 00:29:00.020
I threw all of it away.

00:29:00.020 --> 00:29:02.740
Interestingly, on the day I wrote this letter from Loretto,

00:29:02.740 --> 00:29:04.460
I received an electronic message

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:07.520
that my refill of gliburide was ready.

00:29:07.520 --> 00:29:08.940
I feel fine now.

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:10.460
I use a friend's blood sugar meter

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:11.660
to test my blood each morning

00:29:11.660 --> 00:29:14.900
and it's been between normal and slightly elevated.

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:16.620
What happened to me is minor, though,

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:18.780
compared to what happens to other prisoners.

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:21.900
Systematically across the BOP.

00:29:21.900 --> 00:29:24.700
Ask any federal prisoner anywhere in America

00:29:24.700 --> 00:29:28.360
about medical care and he'll tell you the same thing.

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:31.660
The BOP generally ignores prisoners' medical problems

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:33.620
hoping they'll either go away

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:35.500
or that the prisoner will be released

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:37.940
and will become someone else's problem.

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:40.260
And this isn't just John Kiriakou talking.

00:29:40.260 --> 00:29:42.580
This is a documented policy

00:29:42.580 --> 00:29:45.380
in every BOP facility in America.

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:49.660
I have a friend who was sentenced to two years in prison

00:29:49.660 --> 00:29:52.760
for having an illegal poker machine in his bar.

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:55.020
He suffers from hepatitis C.

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:56.460
As soon as he got to Loretto,

00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:58.820
he told the medical unit about his condition

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:00.340
and that he needed treatment.

00:30:00.340 --> 00:30:03.100
We'll think about it, was the response.

00:30:03.100 --> 00:30:07.580
He asked repeatedly over the next 18 months for treatment.

00:30:07.580 --> 00:30:09.100
He was ignored.

00:30:09.100 --> 00:30:11.340
Finally, two weeks before his release,

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:13.140
he was called to medical.

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:14.980
They would give him the treatment, they said.

00:30:14.980 --> 00:30:17.840
If he would agree to forego his halfway house

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:19.700
and home confinement time.

00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:22.380
In other words, we'll treat you,

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:25.220
but you have to stay in prison for the treatment.

00:30:25.220 --> 00:30:26.140
He declined.

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:29.100
The BOP can now cover itself

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:31.900
by saying that they wanted to treat him,

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:33.380
but he refused.

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:34.980
Well, they knew he would decline.

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:37.420
That was the plan from the very beginning.

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:39.380
That night, he called his wife to tell her

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:40.220
what had happened.

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:41.820
He was upset and angry

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:43.980
and his language reflected that.

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:44.940
Well, the next morning,

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:48.140
he was thrown into solitary for insolence

00:30:48.140 --> 00:30:53.190
where he remained until the day he was released.

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:54.230
A better help add.

00:30:54.230 --> 00:30:57.190
After my session and talking to my therapist

00:30:57.190 --> 00:31:01.030
and really feeling seen and heard for the first time,

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:02.110
I just felt like a weight

00:31:02.110 --> 00:31:03.990
just was lifted off of my shoulders.

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:05.510
And I felt like it was a good match

00:31:05.510 --> 00:31:07.030
from the first time we talked.

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:09.470
I could tell people from lived experience

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:11.150
that they should try therapy.

00:31:11.150 --> 00:31:13.470
I could really be myself again

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:16.630
and I have this continued support with BetterHelp.

