00:31:58.820 --> 00:32:01.540
Judy White is a contributor to Op-Ed News,

00:32:01.540 --> 00:32:06.180
a fantastic website that frequently covers BOP malfeasance.

00:32:06.180 --> 00:32:07.540
Her husband, Gary White,

00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:10.460
is a former Jefferson County, Alabama commissioner

00:32:10.460 --> 00:32:12.740
who was caught up in the Carl Rove instigated

00:32:12.740 --> 00:32:15.460
political case involving former Alabama governor,

00:32:15.460 --> 00:32:16.900
Don Siegelman.

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:18.820
Gary White is serving 10 years

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:21.540
in a federal prison in Forest City, Arkansas.

00:32:21.540 --> 00:32:23.900
Judy White recently told Op-Ed News

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:26.380
about the experience of one of her husband's friends

00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:28.380
at FCI Forest City.

00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:30.140
Here's what she said.

00:32:30.140 --> 00:32:33.380
Quote, today we're very concerned about Dave,

00:32:33.380 --> 00:32:35.580
one of Gary's fellow prisoners.

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:37.260
Dave is 71 years old

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:40.100
and should have already been released to a halfway house.

00:32:40.100 --> 00:32:41.140
For the past two years,

00:32:41.140 --> 00:32:42.580
Dave has sought medical treatment

00:32:42.580 --> 00:32:44.820
because of blood in his urine.

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:47.620
As his condition has become dramatically worse

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:49.820
and Dave has become much sicker,

00:32:49.820 --> 00:32:52.260
his medical needs and efforts to obtain treatment

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:54.340
have been consistently ignored

00:32:54.340 --> 00:32:57.380
or denied by prison employees.

00:32:57.380 --> 00:33:01.540
The BOP routinely and purposely withholds treatment

00:33:01.540 --> 00:33:02.940
then releases sick prisoners

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:05.860
to get whatever help they can get on their own.

00:33:05.860 --> 00:33:08.860
Dave's medical needs became even more urgent

00:33:08.860 --> 00:33:11.780
when he began passing blood clots

00:33:11.820 --> 00:33:13.980
along with lots of blood.

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:16.620
Dave understood that he was in serious trouble

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:17.700
and that prison employees

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:19.860
live by their unofficial motto,

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:21.900
we don't care.

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:24.020
He begged to be taken to the hospital,

00:33:24.020 --> 00:33:27.260
denied and told to go back to his housing unit.

00:33:27.260 --> 00:33:29.780
Dave had no choice but to take a stand.

00:33:29.780 --> 00:33:31.060
He told the prison employees

00:33:31.060 --> 00:33:32.860
that he was not leaving medical

00:33:32.860 --> 00:33:35.700
and that he had to be taken to the hospital.

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:38.180
Eventually, Dave was taken to the hospital

00:33:38.180 --> 00:33:41.420
where it was quickly and easily determined

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:43.460
that he had multiple tumors

00:33:43.460 --> 00:33:47.220
and needed emergency medical intervention and surgery.

00:33:47.220 --> 00:33:49.380
The emergency room physician told Dave

00:33:49.380 --> 00:33:51.340
that if he had not gotten to the hospital

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:55.460
he would have been in the newspaper, the obituaries.

00:33:55.460 --> 00:33:57.220
Wanna guess what happened next?

00:33:57.220 --> 00:33:58.620
Nothing.

00:33:58.620 --> 00:34:00.460
Dave was taken from the hospital,

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:03.180
back to the prison where he remains today

00:34:03.180 --> 00:34:06.740
without surgery and without a plan to save his life.

00:34:07.860 --> 00:34:10.380
The US media have largely ignored

00:34:10.380 --> 00:34:13.220
this abominable denial of medical care.

00:34:13.220 --> 00:34:16.300
It has not, however, been ignored by everybody.

00:34:16.300 --> 00:34:18.260
According to prison legal news,

00:34:18.260 --> 00:34:19.740
the European Court of Human Rights

00:34:19.740 --> 00:34:23.900
refused to extradite suspected terrorist Haroun Rashid Aswat

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:26.220
from the United Kingdom to the United States

00:34:26.220 --> 00:34:29.380
on the grounds that his mental and physical health

00:34:29.380 --> 00:34:31.500
would face significant deterioration

00:34:31.500 --> 00:34:34.380
in the more hostile US prison system.

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:36.340
I have less than 150 days to go

00:34:36.340 --> 00:34:39.300
until my release on February 3rd, 2015.

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:43.060
The first thing I'm going to do is to go see my doctor.

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:45.860
I'm one of the lucky ones, unquote.

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:49.060
That's how I ended that letter from Loretto.

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:51.580
It's funny, had I never been sentenced to Loretto,

00:34:51.580 --> 00:34:53.660
I probably would never have known

00:34:53.660 --> 00:34:55.620
about the terrible problems there

00:34:55.620 --> 00:34:58.020
or throughout the federal prison system.

00:34:58.020 --> 00:35:00.700
It might never have gotten onto my radar,

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:04.540
but once it was on my radar, it had to be dealt with.

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:06.820
The point of whistleblowing is to inform the public

00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:08.580
of something they need to know.

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:10.940
Doing it is dangerous because power

00:35:10.940 --> 00:35:13.380
is incredibly protective of its secrets,

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:16.820
especially as they pertain to power's corruption.

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:19.460
The more experience taught me about the federal prison system,

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:21.380
the more determined I became

00:35:21.380 --> 00:35:25.220
to blow as many whistles as possible on them.

00:35:25.220 --> 00:35:28.020
Even more than that, having been on the inside,

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:30.420
having seen how easy it is to deny

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:32.700
or abuse another citizen's rights,

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:36.540
well, I wasn't just gonna sit there, was I?

00:35:36.580 --> 00:35:38.980
Fyodor Dostoevsky wrote, quote,

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:41.940
a society should be judged not by how it treats

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:43.620
its outstanding citizens,

00:35:43.620 --> 00:35:46.460
but by how it treats its criminals, unquote.

00:35:46.460 --> 00:35:48.740
In America, part of that problem

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:50.740
is who we consider criminal.

00:35:50.740 --> 00:35:52.580
I wrote in another letter from Loretto

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:54.500
about the fact that the United States

00:35:54.500 --> 00:35:57.620
has 5% of the world's population,

00:35:57.620 --> 00:36:02.500
but 25% of the world's prison population.

00:36:02.500 --> 00:36:06.100
How is that a thing in the land of the free?

00:36:07.100 --> 00:36:09.140
The US on a per capita basis

00:36:09.140 --> 00:36:14.140
has more prison inmates than Russia, China, and Iran.

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:16.180
I wrote that, quote,

00:36:16.180 --> 00:36:18.540
this inexcusable fact is a result

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:20.220
of our government's ill-conceived

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:22.540
and ill-advised war on drugs

00:36:22.540 --> 00:36:25.540
and of Congress's propensity in election years

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:29.540
to prove that it is tough on crime, unquote.

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:32.660
Indeed, according to the Congressional Research Service,

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:36.900
Congress has created 50 new crimes per year

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:38.860
over the past 10 years.

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:41.300
Harvard Law School professor, Harvey Silverglate,

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:43.460
argues in his book, Three Felonies a Day,

00:36:43.460 --> 00:36:46.180
that the US is so over-legislated

00:36:46.180 --> 00:36:49.260
and daily life is so over-criminalized

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:50.820
that the average American

00:36:50.820 --> 00:36:52.860
going about his normal business

00:36:52.860 --> 00:36:57.860
on an average day commits three felonies every day.

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:00.500
It's no wonder that the Bureau of Prisons

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:03.380
accounts for a quarter of the Justice Department's budget.

00:37:03.380 --> 00:37:04.700
It's no wonder that according

00:37:04.700 --> 00:37:06.820
to the American Civil Liberties Union,

00:37:06.820 --> 00:37:11.220
there are 3,278 prisoners in the US

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:14.540
serving sentences of life without parole

00:37:14.540 --> 00:37:18.780
for non-violent drug and property crimes.

00:37:18.780 --> 00:37:21.180
In prison from the time you wake up in the morning

00:37:21.180 --> 00:37:22.980
until the time you go to sleep at night,

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:26.140
most people wanna talk to you about their cases.

00:37:26.140 --> 00:37:28.060
At first, it's fascinating.

00:37:28.060 --> 00:37:30.300
After a while, it's tedious.

00:37:30.300 --> 00:37:33.460
Later on, it becomes a real pain.

00:37:33.460 --> 00:37:36.540
But the truth is that many of them have been wronged,

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:38.820
whether by a racist system,

00:37:38.820 --> 00:37:40.780
by incompetent attorneys,

00:37:40.780 --> 00:37:42.780
by overzealous prosecutors,

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:45.020
or by judges whose hands are tied

00:37:45.020 --> 00:37:48.620
because Congress has mandated long minimum sentences,

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:50.820
especially for drug crimes.

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:52.740
At the time, a bipartisan bill

00:37:52.740 --> 00:37:55.300
called the Recidivism Reduction and Public Safety Act,

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:58.060
which was sponsored by Democratic Senator Sheldon Whitehouse

00:37:58.060 --> 00:37:58.980
of Rhode Island,

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:01.660
and Republican Senator John Cornyn of Texas

00:38:01.660 --> 00:38:03.060
was on the table.

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:04.940
So was the US Sentencing Commission vote

00:38:04.940 --> 00:38:07.860
that wanted to lower the drug sentencing guidelines

00:38:07.860 --> 00:38:10.020
for all drug offenses.

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:11.780
The Sentencing Commission's recommendations

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:13.780
would have granted relief to thousands

00:38:13.780 --> 00:38:16.180
of federal prisoners convicted of drug crimes

00:38:16.180 --> 00:38:19.500
before November 1st, 1987.

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:21.980
There were conditions, caveats, and criteria,

00:38:21.980 --> 00:38:24.300
but as I said, thousands of people

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:26.100
serving significant time,

00:38:26.100 --> 00:38:27.980
far beyond what was reasonable,

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:30.700
would have gotten their freedom back.

00:38:30.700 --> 00:38:32.540
I wrote in another letter from Loretto,

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:35.540
quote, these are not nameless, faceless people

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:37.220
that we're talking about.

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:39.100
There's a human side to this.

00:38:39.100 --> 00:38:41.820
We're talking about people who made a mistake,

00:38:41.820 --> 00:38:43.660
usually in their youth,

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:46.660
and are paying for it with their lives.

00:38:46.660 --> 00:38:48.900
Their families are also victims,

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:50.620
rather than being in prison

00:38:50.620 --> 00:38:53.300
where each prisoner costs the American taxpayer

00:38:53.300 --> 00:38:58.140
$29,291.25 per year.

00:38:58.140 --> 00:39:00.580
That number is far higher today, by the way.

00:39:01.660 --> 00:39:04.060
They could be out working, paying taxes,

00:39:04.060 --> 00:39:07.100
paying child support, and contributing to society.

00:39:07.980 --> 00:39:10.740
Both the Recidivism Reduction and Public Safety Act

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:12.580
and the Smarter Sentencing Act

00:39:12.580 --> 00:39:16.580
died in the Senate without even so much as a vote.

00:39:16.580 --> 00:39:18.900
That was because then-majority leader,

00:39:18.900 --> 00:39:22.660
Democrat Harry Reid, never brought it up for a vote.

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:25.380
When power changed hands and Republican Mitch McConnell

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:27.020
became the Senate-majority leader,

00:39:27.020 --> 00:39:30.900
he said the bills would no longer even be considered,

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:33.220
and that's where it all remains to this day.

00:39:34.820 --> 00:39:37.980
Even as my efforts to get early release were fizzling,

00:39:37.980 --> 00:39:40.300
a new guy moved across the hall from me.

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:42.860
He was a heroin dealer who sold tainted junk

00:39:42.860 --> 00:39:45.260
to a friend, resulting in the friend's death.

00:39:45.260 --> 00:39:47.380
The heroin dealer got 20 years in prison

00:39:47.380 --> 00:39:48.700
and was gri-

00:39:48.700 --> 00:39:50.860
The heroin dealer got 20 years in prison

00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:54.500
and was given a greatest severity security classification,

00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:55.900
the very same as mine.

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:58.860
The heroin dealer appealed the classification,

00:39:58.860 --> 00:40:00.180
just like I did,

00:40:00.180 --> 00:40:02.940
arguing that he was not a threat to society

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:05.340
and should be in a minimum security camp.

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:08.100
Well, he won his appeal.

00:40:08.100 --> 00:40:10.580
His security classification was downgraded

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:13.540
and he was transferred across the street to the camp,

00:40:13.540 --> 00:40:15.140
to where I should have been.

00:40:15.140 --> 00:40:16.220
To the Bureau of Prisons,

00:40:16.220 --> 00:40:19.940
I was far more dangerous than a drug dealing murderer.

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:23.620
In our next episode, we'll talk to Paul Wright,

00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:25.500
co-founder of The Prison Legal News,

00:40:25.500 --> 00:40:27.940
the longest-running newspaper produced by and for

00:40:27.940 --> 00:40:30.540
current and former prisoners in U.S. history,

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:32.540
and we'll get his profound insights

00:40:32.540 --> 00:40:34.580
into this persistent problem.

00:40:34.580 --> 00:40:36.820
In the meantime, thanks for listening.

00:40:36.820 --> 00:40:39.980
Please don't forget to like, rate, review, comment on,

00:40:39.980 --> 00:40:42.620
and most importantly, share the podcast.

00:40:42.620 --> 00:40:44.900
Some secrets you have to keep,

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:47.100
others you have to tell the world.

00:40:48.100 --> 00:40:50.540
Until next time, I'm John Kiriakou.

